--TEST--
A `callable` naming a `void` member runs it and answers `null`, over a static target, an instance one and a `Core` member alike — `rule:types/callable-values`, `rule:types/anonymous-function` and `rule:types/implicit-capture`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/anonymous-function` makes `callable` the only function type, so a `void` member's
// `(...)` produces the same object every other one produces and is called the
// same way. What it hands *back* is the question this case asks. A `void`
// frame returns nothing at all, so the caller reads the null its own out slot
// started as — one answer, rather than a second no-value representation for a
// program to test for.
class Counter {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor() {
        $this->n = 0;
    }

    public function bump(int $by): void {
        $this->n = $this->n + $by;
    }

    public static function announce(string $what): void {
        echo "announce ", $what, "\n";
    }
}

// A static `void` target: the called class is baked in, the body runs, and
// nothing comes back.
callable $announce = Counter::announce(...);
mixed $said = $announce("open");
echo $said == null ? "null\n" : "not null\n";

// An instance `void` target captures its receiver by value (`rule:types/implicit-capture`), so
// what the call mutates is the object the `(...)` saw — a `void` member is
// called for exactly that, and losing it would be invisible in the answer.
Counter $c = new Counter();
callable $bump = $c->bump(...);
mixed $bumped = $bump(4);
$bump(3);
echo $c->n, "\n";
echo $bumped == null ? "null\n" : "not null\n";

// A Tier 0 `Core` member is native Rust behind a helper symbol, which is the
// thunk's *other* calling convention — it borrows what the thunk staged rather
// than taking it — and it answers the same nothing.
Core\ObjectSet<int> $set = new Core\ObjectSet<int>();
callable $add = $set->add(...);
mixed $added = $add(7);
$add(9);
echo $set->count(), "\n";
echo $added == null ? "null\n" : "not null\n";
--EXPECT--
announce open
null
7
null
2
null
